<section style="padding-top:40px">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px">

    <div class="guide-toc">
      <h4>In this guide</h4>
      <a href="#purpose">What handicap competition is trying to do</a>
      <a href="#pairing">Pairing and handicapping are separate levers</a>
      <a href="#rating">Start from an honest strength estimate</a>
      <a href="#mapping">The rating-to-points mapping is the hard part</a>
      <a href="#validate">How to validate your handicap scale</a>
      <a href="#freeze">Freeze the handicaps for the event</a>
      <a href="#format">Choose a format from court capacity</a>
      <a href="#groups">Seeding groups without double-compensating</a>
      <a href="#running">Run matches as courts become available</a>
      <a href="#exceptions">Substitutions, no-shows and errors</a>
      <a href="#trust">Explain enough maths - but not too much</a>
      <a href="#epegboard">Using ePegboard competitions</a>
      <a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:34px">

      <p class="lead"><strong>A handicap should remove some of the predictable advantage of being stronger, not manufacture a predetermined upset.</strong> The strong pair should still be rewarded for playing well; the developing pair should start close enough that playing well can matter.</p>

      <p>That makes handicap badminton a model, not a magic trick. You estimate the pair strengths, convert the difference into a starting advantage, then eventually check whether the conversion did what you intended.</p>

      <h2 id="purpose">What handicap competition is trying to do</h2>

      <p>Scratch competition answers:</p>
      <p><em>Who is strongest under these conditions?</em></p>

      <p>Handicap competition asks something different:</p>
      <p><em>Who performs best relative to the advantage we expected them to have?</em></p>

      <p>That can make an internal tournament much more open. The strongest pair cannot coast through early matches; a developing pair has a route to the knockout; and matches between players who would rarely meet in normal competition become meaningful.</p>

      <p>But "inclusive" is not the same as "automatically fair". If your starting ratings are poor or your handicap curve is too aggressive, the event simply swaps one systematic advantage for another.</p>

      <h2 id="pairing">Pairing and handicapping are separate levers</h2>

      <p>Before calculating a single head start, decide how pairs are formed.</p>

      <p>You have three broad choices:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>self-selected pairs:</strong> socially simple, but pair strengths may vary enormously;</li>
        <li><strong>seeded/balanced pairing:</strong> combine stronger and developing players so pair totals are closer;</li>
        <li><strong>random pairs:</strong> fun and transparent, but can create extreme combinations by chance.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Then the handicap deals with the strength difference that remains.</p>

      <p>Do not confuse the two. If you carefully engineer every pair to the same estimated strength and then also apply a large handicap, you may be compensating twice. If you let any pair form freely, the handicap has much more work to do.</p>

      <p>A popular club format is to split entrants into stronger and developing halves and draw one from each. That usually narrows pair-strength spread while preserving some surprise. It is a design choice, not a law of handicap badminton.</p>

      <h2 id="rating">Start from an honest strength estimate</h2>

      <p>The handicap cannot be better than the number underneath it.</p>

      <p>A/B/C grades can work for a small event, but a grade boundary creates jumps: the strongest B and weakest B get treated alike until one is suddenly called A.</p>

      <p>A continuous rating gives finer input, especially if it comes from many recent doubles results. Even then, remember what it is estimating:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>new players are uncertain;</li>
        <li>club-specific ratings may not compare directly across disconnected clubs;</li>
        <li>individual ratings do not fully capture partnership chemistry;</li>
        <li>a player returning from injury may be misrepresented;</li>
        <li>singles strength and doubles strength are not interchangeable.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>If an organiser knows that a rating is plainly stale, fix the input <em>before</em> publishing the draw. Quietly changing the handicap after the player wins their first match is far harder to defend.</p>

      <h2 id="mapping">The rating-to-points mapping is the hard part</h2>

      <p>Suppose Pair A is estimated stronger than Pair B by 300 rating points. How many rally points is that worth in a game to 21?</p>

      <p>There is no universal badminton law that answers this. A rating model predicts some notion of expected performance; a handicap system must translate that into the altered race to the target score.</p>

      <p>A workable handicap curve should have four properties:</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>monotonic:</strong> a larger strength gap never gives a smaller advantage;</li>
        <li><strong>smooth:</strong> a tiny rating difference near a threshold should not suddenly add three starting points;</li>
        <li><strong>bounded:</strong> extreme mismatches should hit a maximum rather than produce absurd starts;</li>
        <li><strong>calibrated:</strong> real results should roughly match the competitive probability you intended.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>The last one is where many club handicap tables are weak. They were inherited from somebody's spreadsheet years ago and nobody has checked whether +4 still means what the club thinks it means.</p>

      <h2 id="validate">How to validate your handicap scale</h2>

      <p>Do not tune a handicap because one 1900-rated player complains after losing to a 1400.</p>

      <p>Upsets are the point.</p>

      <p>Instead, collect enough matches and group them by the <strong>pre-handicap strength gap</strong>. For example:</p>

      <div class="cmp-wrap">
        <table class="cmp-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Gap band</th><th>Matches</th><th>Average handicap</th><th>Stronger pair win %</th><th>Average final margin</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>0-99</td><td>…</td><td>…</td><td>…</td><td>…</td></tr>
            <tr><td>100-199</td><td>…</td><td>…</td><td>…</td><td>…</td></tr>
            <tr><td>200-399</td><td>…</td><td>…</td><td>…</td><td>…</td></tr>
            <tr><td>400+</td><td>…</td><td>…</td><td>…</td><td>…</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p style="margin-top:18px">If the stronger pair wins 80% of matches after handicap in one band, your curve is probably under-compensating there <em>if</em> your stated objective is near-even contests. If the weaker side wins 70%, it may be over-compensating.</p>

      <p>Also examine margin. A 52% win rate with most matches ending by ten points suggests the event is volatile in a different way from a 52% win rate full of 21-19s.</p>

      <p>Do not expect exact 50%. Sample sizes will be small and players are not identical probability distributions. You are looking for systematic bias, not proof.</p>

      <h2 id="freeze">Freeze the handicaps for the event</h2>

      <p>This is one of the cleanest rules you can make.</p>

      <p>Take the ratings or grades at a stated cut-off, calculate every pair strength and handicap from that snapshot, then freeze them for the tournament.</p>

      <p>Your underlying rating system may still record tournament matches. But do not let Match 1 change the handicap for Match 2 unless the event was explicitly designed as an adaptive handicap competition.</p>

      <p>Otherwise schedule order becomes part of the competition: two identical pairs could receive different head starts against the same opponent because one happened to play first.</p>

      <p>Correct obvious data-entry errors early and openly. Do not "correct" a handicap merely because the first result surprised you.</p>

      <h2 id="format">Choose a format from court capacity</h2>

      <p>Handicap does not change tournament arithmetic.</p>

      <p>Groups of four pairs are particularly manageable because each group contains:</p>

      <p style="text-align:center;font-size:1.08rem"><strong>4 × 3 ÷ 2 = 6 group matches</strong></p>

      <p>Four groups therefore create 24 group matches. If the top two from each group enter an eight-pair knockout, the knockout adds seven matches: four quarter-finals, two semi-finals and a final. Total: <strong>31 matches</strong>.</p>

      <p>On four courts, 31 matches require at least eight court waves even before participant conflicts and changeovers. At a 15-minute match slot, the raw lower bound is about two hours. That tells you immediately whether best-of-three to 21 is fantasy.</p>

      <p>Choose match scoring from the capacity:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>one game to 21 for a compact but recognisable badminton game;</li>
        <li>a shorter target for a very dense social event;</li>
        <li>best of three when the field is small enough for match depth to matter more than throughput.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Leave margin. Knockout matches have a habit of becoming the ones everybody watches and nobody wants rushed.</p>

      <h2 id="groups">Seeding groups without double-compensating</h2>

      <p>If pair strengths still vary substantially, spread the strongest estimated pairs across groups. This reduces the chance of one group containing three likely finalists while another has none.</p>

      <p>But remember: the handicaps themselves are designed to reduce strength advantage. Heavy seeding plus aggressive handicapping can make the underlying ratings matter surprisingly little.</p>

      <p>That is not necessarily bad. Decide what the event is trying to reward.</p>

      <p>If you want the strongest pair to retain some advantage while making matches competitive, seed groups conventionally and use moderate handicaps. If the event is deliberately a leveller where anybody should have close to the same chance, the handicap can do more of the work and seeding matters mainly for variety.</p>

      <h2 id="running">Run matches as courts become available</h2>

      <p>Do not force every court to wait for the slowest match in "Round 2" if the next legal fixtures can start.</p>

      <p>Keep a ready list of group matches whose pairs are free. As a court opens, send the next one. That keeps capacity high and avoids punishing everybody because one game reaches 29-29.</p>

      <p>Publish three things clearly:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>the pairings and their fixed ratings/strengths;</li>
        <li>the head start for each specific match;</li>
        <li>live group standings / knockout state.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Players should never be negotiating whether the start was +5 or +6 while standing on court.</p>

      <h2 id="exceptions">Substitutions, no-shows and errors</h2>

      <p><strong>No-show before first match:</strong> replacing the player can be cleaner than awarding a day of walkovers, but recalculate the pair strength before play and tell the group.</p>

      <p><strong>Injury after matches have been played:</strong> replacement is harder because the pair is now a different competitor. Options include withdrawal, emergency substitution with a recalculated handicap, or allowing the remaining player to continue only under a predefined rule. Choose before the event.</p>

      <p><strong>Group withdrawal:</strong> decide whether completed results stand or are expunged. If qualification depends on points difference, retaining some opponents' matches and giving others walkovers can skew the group.</p>

      <p><strong>Wrong starting rating:</strong> if it is a clear administrative error, correct it before the pair's first match where possible. After several matches, changing it can invalidate the competitive basis of the group.</p>

      <p><strong>Wrong score entered:</strong> allow correction and ensure any dependent standings/bracket are recalculated.</p>

      <h2 id="trust">Explain enough maths - but not too much</h2>

      <p>Players do not need a lecture on logistic functions before warm-up.</p>

      <p>They do need to know:</p>

      <ol>
        <li>where their starting rating came from;</li>
        <li>how the two player ratings become a pair strength;</li>
        <li>that a larger pair gap creates a larger head start;</li>
        <li>the maximum handicap;</li>
        <li>that the numbers are frozen for the event;</li>
        <li>and what happens if a starting rating is plainly wrong.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>Publish the actual conversion table or formula somewhere for anyone who wants it. Transparency does not require making everybody read it.</p>

      <h2 id="epegboard">Using ePegboard competitions</h2>

      <p><a href="/features/competitions">ePegboard's current handicap competition workflow</a> starts from club members and their ratings, lets the organiser create or adjust pairs, supports groups of three to five pairs, and runs a round-robin group stage followed by a knockout. Pair strength drives a weaker-side starting advantage, currently capped at +10 for a standard game to 21.</p>

      <p>The top two from each group can progress to the knockout, with group winners kept apart in the first knockout round. Scores update standings live; if a result is corrected later, dependent knockout state is reset so the bracket remains consistent.</p>

      <p>That removes the spreadsheet work. It does not remove the design responsibility described above: starting ratings still need to be credible, the chosen field still has to fit the court booking, and players should understand what the handicap is trying to achieve.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout">
        <h4>The test of a handicap system</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">Not “did a weaker pair win?” They should sometimes. Ask whether, across enough matches, the pre-handicap strength gap stops being a strong predictor of who wins. If it remains dominant, the handicap is not doing enough; if the relationship reverses, it is doing too much.</p>
      </div>

